https://math.stanford.edu/~vakil/216blog/FOAGoct2125public.pdf, Vakil's algebraic geometry
	- (2.2A) Presheaf is contravariant functor. (Hint: Restriction maps of sets as the reverse of the inclusions. Self restriction gives same set. Hence, contravariant functor.)
	- (2.2B) Presheaf of bounded functions not sheaf. Presheaf admitting holomorphic square root not sheaf. (Hint: fails global bounded function, fails global holomorphic square root due to winding number. Repair with sheafification.)
	- (2.2C) Identity and gluability axioms as limit. (Hint: Limit of Cech cocycle equalizer is section.)
	- (2.2D) Verify examples are sheaves? (Hint: Separation and global gluing via gluing of charts due to definition of manifold. For real continuous functions, think of them as covers or arrows, same gluing and separation applies.)
	- (2.2E) Locally constant sheaf (local systems) is sheaf. (Hint: locally isomorphic to a sheafified constant PREsheaf, satisfies sheaf axioms. Constant presheaf fails separatedness on empty set and require sheafification. Note consistency of definition needed)
	- (2.2F) Verify sheaf axioms for sheaf of continuous functions of topological spaces, arbitrary topology.
	- (8.3.J) Finite morphisms have finite fibers. (Hint: Check local on a base. From finite morphism, finitely generated as R-module, fiber over point, finite dimensional module over field is Artinian admitting finite spectrum, so finite morphisms)
	- (11.2.13) Intersection of two affine opens is affine opens using separatedness. (Hint: Separated implies semiseparated, separated means diagonal map is closed immersion and intersection is realised over diagonal.)
	- (17.3.B) Finite morphisms are projective. (Hint: projective local on base, pick Y = Spec R, X = Spec A, where A finitely generated as R-module from finiteness which surjects into A giving the closed immersion to compose with standard open immersion into projective space embedding X as subscheme of projective space, X -> Y proper from finiteness, P^n_Y -> Y is separateed, X to P^n_Y is closed immersion.)
	- (18.1.A) Pushforward of coherent sheaves by projective morphisms are coherent. (Hint: Immediate from Grothendieck's coherence theorem by cheating using projective morphism is proper and reduced target to . Coherence is finite type with finitely presented kernel. Reduce case to Noetherian ring using locality in definition of coherence. By Vakil 16.2.1. pushforward of quasicoherent sheaf along projective morphism is quasicoherent, proof is by checking locally since quasicoherence, covered by affine opens, so morphism is qcqs, preimage is qcqs scheme, restriction is quasicoherent sheaf on U, take affine cover, global sections, restriction pushforward is locally isomorphic to R-module. I could not do this without applying the theorem?)
	- (18.1.C) Exact sequence of quasicoherent sheaf, 0 -> F -> G -> H -> 0 of quasicoherent sheaves on X, projective over A with F coherent, show exact sequence of H^0. (Hint: reduce to proving for n >> 0, H^1(X, F(n)) = 0. Choose finite open cover of X using X projective hence quasicompact, get short exact sequence of complex and long exact sequence in cohomology. Follows from Serre vanishing by considering twisting with integer n using F coherent.)

https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/07JV, the Snake Lemma in abelian categories
    - State the Snake Lemma: given a commutative diagram with exact rows in an abelian category, construct the natural connecting homomorphism $\delta: \ker(c) \to \operatorname{coker}(a)$ yielding the six-term exact sequence $0 \to \ker(a) \to \ker(b) \to \ker(c) \xrightarrow{\delta} \operatorname{coker}(a) \to \operatorname{coker}(b) \to \operatorname{coker}(c) \to 0$.

https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0517, finite modules and finitely presented modules
    - Prove that an $R$-module $M$ is finitely presented if and only if it is finitely generated and the module of relations among the generators is finitely generated ($R^m \to R^n \to M \to 0$).
    - (Lemma 10.5.3) Prove that in a short exact sequence $0 \to M_1 \to M_2 \to M_3 \to 0$, if $M_1$ and $M_3$ are finitely generated (resp. finitely presented), then so is $M_2$.

https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00F2, ring maps of finite type and finite presentation
    - Define a ring map $R \to S$ to be of finite type if $S \cong R[x_1, \dots, x_n]/I$, and of finite presentation if $I$ is finitely generated ($S \cong R[x_1, \dots, x_n]/(f_1, \dots, f_m)$).

https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0562, finite ring maps

https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/07N7, colimits
    - (Lemma 10.8.2) Construct the directed colimit $\varinjlim M_i$ of a filtered system of $R$-modules as a quotient $\bigoplus M_i / \sim$.

https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00CM, localisation
    - Construct the localization $S^{-1} A$ of a ring $A$ with respect to a multiplicative subset $S$, and prove its universal property.
    - Prove that localization is an exact functor: $S^{-1}(0 \to M' \to M \to M'' \to 0)$ remains exact.

https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00CV, tensor products
    - (Lemma 10.12.2) Construct the tensor product of modules $M \otimes_R N$ and prove its universal property for $R$-bilinear maps.
    - (Lemma 10.12.8) State and prove the Tensor-Hom adjunction: $\operatorname{Hom}_R(M \otimes_R N, P) \cong \operatorname{Hom}_R(M, \operatorname{Hom}_R(N, P))$.
    - Prove that the tensor product functor $-\otimes_R N$ is right exact and preserves all colimits.

https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/05G3, base change
    - (Definition 10.14.1) Define the base change of an $R$-algebra $S$ along $R \to R'$ via the tensor product $S' = S \otimes_R R'$.

https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00H9, flat modules and flat ring maps
    - Define a flat $R$-module $M$ by the exactness of the functor $-\otimes_R M$, and a faithfully flat module by the property that $-\otimes_R M$ is exact and reflects non-zero modules.
    - (Lemma 10.39.2) Prove that for a flat module $M$ and ideals $I, J \subseteq R$, $(I \cap J)M = IM \cap JM$.

https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/07BH, local rings
    - Define a local ring $(R, \mathfrak{m}, k)$ and show that $x \in R$ is a unit if and only if $x \notin \mathfrak{m}$.

https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00DR, prime avoidance and algebraic lemmas

https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/07RC, Nakayama's lemma
    - Prove Nakayama's Lemma: if $M$ is a finitely generated $R$-module and $I M = M$ for $I \subseteq \operatorname{Jac}(R)$, then $M = 0$.

https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00FM, Noetherian rings
    - (Lemma 10.31.1) State the Hilbert Basis Theorem: if $R$ is Noetherian, then the polynomial ring $R[X]$ and power series ring $R[[X]]$ are Noetherian.

https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0594, Mittag-Leffler systems
    - Define the Mittag-Leffler condition for an inverse system of abelian groups $(A_i, \phi_{ij})$, and prove that if $(A_i)$ is Mittag-Leffler, then $\varprojlim^1 A_i = 0$.

https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00LO, Ext groups
    - Define $\operatorname{Ext}_R^n(M, N)$ as the right derived functors of $\operatorname{Hom}_R(M, -)$, classifying $n$-fold extensions.

https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00LY, Tor groups and flatness
    - Define $\operatorname{Tor}_n^R(M, N)$ as the left derived functors of $M \otimes_R -$, and show that $\operatorname{Tor}_1^R(M, N) = 0$ for flat modules.

https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0AUH, regular sequences
    - Define an $M$-regular sequence $(x_1, \dots, x_n)$ on an $R$-module $M$: $x_i$ is a non-zerodivisor on $M/(x_1, \dots, x_{i-1})M$ for all $1 \le i \le n$.

https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00LE, depth of an ideal in a finite R-module
    - Define the depth $\operatorname{depth}_I(M)$ as the maximal length of an $M$-regular sequence in $I$.

