https://lawverearchives.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/12/1970-quantifiers-and-sheaves.pdf, Lawvere and Tierney's Quantifiers and Sheaves
    - Define an elementary topos as a Cartesian closed category with finite limits and a subobject classifier $\Omega$.
    - Compute the subobject classifier $\Omega$ for the topos of sheaves $\operatorname{Sh}(X)$ on a topological space.

https://www.math.cmu.edu/~af1p/Teaching/Combinatorics/Slides/Basic-Counting.pdf, basic counting and combinatorics
    - (Page 2) Compute the number of functions $[n] \to [m]$ as $m^n$, and subsets of $[n]$ as $2^n$.
    - (Page 5) Show that the number of even subsets of a non-empty finite set equals the number of odd subsets ($2^{n-1}$).
    - Compute the binomial coefficients $\binom{n}{k} = \frac{n!}{k!(n-k)!}$ and Pascal's identity $\binom{n}{k} = \binom{n-1}{k} + \binom{n-1}{k-1}$.
    - State Vandermonde's convolution formula: $\sum_{r=0}^k \binom{m}{r}\binom{n}{k-r} = \binom{m+n}{k}$.

https://www.math.stonybrook.edu/~kamenova/homepage_files/Hartshorne_engl.pdf, Hartshorne chapter 1.1 to 1.7
    - (Example 1.1.1) Classify the closed algebraic sets of $\mathbb{A}^1(k)$ over an algebraically closed field as finite sets of points or $\mathbb{A}^1(k)$ itself.
    - (Proposition 1.5) Prove that every algebraic set $Y$ in a Noetherian topological space decomposes uniquely as a finite union of irreducible components $Y = Y_1 \cup \dots \cup Y_r$.
    - (Theorem 1.8A) Prove that the dimension of an affine variety $Y$ equals the transcendence degree of its function field $K(Y)$ over $k$, and $\dim \mathbb{A}^n = n$.
    - (Exercise 2.14) Define the Segre embedding $\psi: \mathbb{P}^n \times \mathbb{P}^m \to \mathbb{P}^{nm+n+m}$ and compute its defining equations.
    - (Proposition 2.2) Prove that the standard projective charts $U_i = \{x_i \ne 0\} \subset \mathbb{P}^n$ are homeomorphic to affine space $\mathbb{A}^n$.
    - (Theorem 5.1) Prove that a point $P$ on an affine variety $Y \subset \mathbb{A}^n$ is non-singular if and only if the local ring $\mathcal{O}_{Y, P}$ is a regular local ring ($\dim_{\kappa(P)} \mathfrak{m}/\mathfrak{m}^2 = \dim Y$).
    - (Theorem 7.7) State Bézout's Theorem: for projective curves $Y, Z \subset \mathbb{P}^2$ of degrees $d, e$ intersecting transversely, $\sum_{P \in Y \cap Z} i(Y, Z; P) = d \cdot e$.

https://www.math.purdue.edu/~jlipman/MA598/Serre-Course%20in%20Arithmetic.pdf, Serre's A Course in Arithmetic, Chapters I to VI
    - (Chapter I, Theorem 2) Compute the order of the multiplicative group of a finite field: $|\mathbb{F}_q^\times| = q - 1$.
    - (Chapter I, Theorem 3) State the Chevalley-Warning Theorem: if $f_\alpha \in \mathbb{F}_q[X_1, \dots, X_n]$ satisfy $\sum \deg(f_\alpha) < n$, then the number of common zeros $|V|$ is $0 \pmod p$.
    - (Chapter I, Theorem 4) Define the Legendre symbol $\left(\frac{x}{p}\right) \equiv x^{(p-1)/2} \pmod p$ for $x \in \mathbb{F}_p^\times$.
    - (Chapter I, Theorem 5) State the Law of Quadratic Reciprocity: $\left(\frac{p}{q}\right)\left(\frac{q}{p}\right) = (-1)^{\frac{p-1}{2}\frac{q-1}{2}}$ for odd primes $p, q$, and compute the Gauss sum proof.
    - (Chapter II) Define the field of $p$-adic numbers $\mathbb{Q}_p$ and the ring of $p$-adic integers $\mathbb{Z}_p = \varprojlim \mathbb{Z}/p^n\mathbb{Z}$.
    - (Chapter III) Define the Hilbert symbol $(a, b)_v \in \{\pm 1\}$ over local fields and prove Hilbert's reciprocity law $\prod_v (a, b)_v = 1$.
    - (Chapter IV) State the Hasse-Minkowski Theorem: a quadratic form over $\mathbb{Q}$ has a non-trivial zero in $\mathbb{Q}$ if and only if it has a non-trivial zero in $\mathbb{R}$ and in $\mathbb{Q}_p$ for all primes $p$.

https://prclare.people.wm.edu/m410f23/Serre_Complex_semisimple_Lie_algebras.pdf, Serre's Complex Semisimple Lie Algebras, Chapters 1 to 3
    - (Chapter 1) Define nilpotent Lie algebras via the lower central series $C^{n+1}\mathfrak{g} = [\mathfrak{g}, C^n\mathfrak{g}]$ and solvable Lie algebras via the derived series $D^{n+1}\mathfrak{g} = [D^n\mathfrak{g}, D^n\mathfrak{g}]$.
    - (Chapter 1, Theorem 1) State Engel's Theorem: a finite-dimensional Lie algebra $\mathfrak{g}$ is nilpotent if and only if $\operatorname{ad}(x)$ is nilpotent for all $x \in \mathfrak{g}$.
    - (Chapter 1, Theorem 2) State Lie's Theorem: every finite-dimensional representation of a solvable Lie algebra over $\mathbb{C}$ has a simultaneous eigenvector.
    - (Chapter 2) Define the Killing form $B(x, y) = \operatorname{Tr}(\operatorname{ad}(x)\operatorname{ad}(y))$ and state Cartan's criterion for semisimplicity ($B$ is non-degenerate).
    - (Chapter 3) Define Cartan subalgebras $\mathfrak{h} \subset \mathfrak{g}$ as nilpotent self-normalizing subalgebras, and decompose $\mathfrak{g} = \mathfrak{h} \oplus \bigoplus_{\alpha \in \Phi} \mathfrak{g}_\alpha$ into root spaces.

https://webhomes.maths.ed.ac.uk/~v1ranick/papers/milnortop.pdf, Milnor's Topology from a Differentiable Viewpoint
    - Define regular and critical values of a smooth map $f: M \to N$.
    - State Sard's Theorem: the set of critical values of a smooth map has Lebesgue measure zero.
    - Define the Brouwer degree $\deg(f) \pmod 2$ and integer degree $\deg(f) = \sum_{x \in f^{-1}(y)} \operatorname{sgn}(df_x)$ for maps between compact oriented manifolds of the same dimension.
    - Classify connected 1-dimensional smooth manifolds as the circle $S^1$ or the real line $\mathbb{R}$.

https://www.math.auckland.ac.nz/~hekmati/Books/BottTu.pdf, Bott and Tu, differential topology

https://opus.bibliothek.uni-augsburg.de/opus4/files/42513/notes.pdf, Blechschmidt's thesis on the internal language of schemes
    - (Page 11) Explain the internal translation in $\operatorname{Sh}(X)$: an $\mathcal{O}_X$-module $\mathcal{F}$ is of finite type if and only if it is internally a finitely generated module over the ring object $\mathcal{O}_X$.
    - (Page 19) Translate Kripke-Joyal semantics for open sets $U \subseteq X$: $U \Vdash \phi \land \psi \iff (U \Vdash \phi \text{ and } U \Vdash \psi)$, and $U \Vdash \exists x \, \phi(x) \iff$ there is an open cover $U = \bigcup U_i$ with sections $s_i \in \mathcal{F}(U_i)$ such that $U_i \Vdash \phi(s_i)$.
    - (Page 28) Formulate the internal property that $\mathcal{O}_X$ is a local ring: $1 \ne 0$ and $x + y \text{ is invertible} \implies (x \text{ is invertible} \lor y \text{ is invertible})$.

https://www.mimuw.edu.pl/~gael/xxi/files/slides_posters/Blechschmidt.pdf, Blechschmidt's slides on the internal language of schemes
    - (Slide 19) Translate finite presentation and finite freeness of modules into internal language over the Zariski topos $\operatorname{Zar}(S)$.
    - (Slide 27) Show that any finitely generated module over an internal field in $\operatorname{Zar}(S)$ is internally not-not free (generic freeness).

https://math.berkeley.edu/~reb/courses/256A/256A.pdf, Borcherds algebraic geometry notes
    - Solve $x^2 + y^2 = z^2$ in $\mathbb{Z}$ by factoring $x^2 = (z - y)(z + y)$ into coprime factors to obtain primitive Pythagorean triples.

https://alainconnes.org/wp-content/uploads/segals-GammaRings.pdf, Segal's Gamma rings
    - Compute the extension of the field with one element $\mathbb{F}_1$ to $\mathbb{Z}$ via Borger's $\Lambda$-rings.

https://www.cambridge.org/core/books/galois-groups-and-fundamental-groups/2511B1C10ACF174A0F444A045D9C1F89, Galois groups and fundamental groups
    - State Grothendieck's Galois-étale correspondence: the category of finite étale coverings of a connected scheme $X$ is anti-equivalent to the category of finite sets with a continuous action of the étale fundamental group $\pi_1^{\text{ét}}(X, \bar{x})$.
    - Prove that every finite separable extension $L/k$ is generated by a single element (Primitive Element Theorem).
    - State the Artin-Schreier theorem for cyclic extensions of degree $p$ in characteristic $p$ via $x^p - x = a$.
    - Compute the absolute Galois group $\operatorname{Gal}(\mathbb{F}_q^{\mathrm{sep}}/\mathbb{F}_q) \cong \widehat{\mathbb{Z}}$ generated by the Frobenius automorphism $x \mapsto x^q$.

https://www.math.ias.edu/~bhatt/math/etalestcksproj.pdf, Bhatt's notes on etale topology
    - Define unramified and étale morphisms of schemes via the vanishing and freeness of the module of relative Kähler differentials $\Omega_{X/Y}^1 = 0$.

https://math.mit.edu/classes/18.783/2025/LectureSlides4.pdf, elliptic curves lecture 4
    - (Page 3) Compute the group law formulas for addition and doubling on a Weierstrass elliptic curve $y^2 = x^3 + A x + B$.
    - (Page 10) Define the $q$-th power Frobenius endomorphism $\pi_q: E \to E$ by $(x, y) \mapsto (x^q, y^q)$ and show $\deg(\pi_q) = q$.
    - (Page 13) Define isogenies $\alpha: E_1 \to E_2$ as non-constant regular group homomorphisms, and decompose them into separable and purely inseparable parts $\alpha = \alpha_{\mathrm{sep}} \circ \pi_p^r$.

https://math.mit.edu/classes/18.783/2025/LectureSlides5.pdf, elliptic curves lecture 5
    - (Page 2) Prove that every isogeny $\alpha: E_1 \to E_2$ has a finite kernel with $|\ker(\alpha)| = \deg_s(\alpha)$.
    - (Page 5) Show that the degree map is multiplicative under composition of isogenies: $\deg(\alpha \circ \beta) = \deg(\alpha)\deg(\beta)$.

https://math.mit.edu/classes/18.783/2025/LectureSlides6.pdf, elliptic curves lecture 6
    - (Page 3) Compute the degree of the multiplication-by-$n$ map: $\deg([n]) = n^2$.
    - (Page 3) Compute the $n$-torsion subgroup $E[n] \cong \mathbb{Z}/n\mathbb{Z} \times \mathbb{Z}/n\mathbb{Z}$ for $\gcd(n, p) = 1$, and $E[p^r] \cong \mathbb{Z}/p^r\mathbb{Z}$ (ordinary) or $0$ (supersingular).

https://math.mit.edu/classes/18.783/2025/LectureSlides7.pdf, elliptic curves lecture 7
    - (Page 2) Define the dual isogeny $\widehat{\alpha}: E_2 \to E_1$ satisfying $\widehat{\alpha} \circ \alpha = [\deg \alpha]$, and compute the trace of an isogeny $\operatorname{Tr}(\alpha) = \alpha + \widehat{\alpha}$.
    - (Page 3) State Hasse's Theorem: the trace of Frobenius satisfies $|a_p| \le 2\sqrt{p}$ with $|E(\mathbb{F}_p)| = p + 1 - a_p$.

