    - (Page 2) Verify the field axioms and vector space axioms over a field $K$.
    - (Page 7) Define the subspace $K^{(I)} \subseteq K^I$ of functions with finite support $\operatorname{supp}(f) = \{i \in I \mid f(i) \ne 0\}$.
    - (Page 8) Define the transpose of an $m \times n$ matrix $A^T \in M_{nm}(K)$ and show $(A B)^T = B^T A^T$.
    - (Page 12) State the Rank-Nullity Theorem: for any linear map $f: V \to W$, $\dim V = \dim \ker(f) + \operatorname{rank}(f)$.
    - (Page 13) Define elementary row operations and their corresponding invertible elementary matrices in Reduced Row Echelon Form (RREF).
    - (Page 20) Prove that any two bases of a finite-dimensional vector space have the same cardinality, defining $\dim_K(V)$.
    - (Page 25) Prove Grassmann's formula: $\dim(W_1 + W_2) = \dim W_1 + \dim W_2 - \dim(W_1 \cap W_2)$.
    - (Page 41) Define the dual space $V^* = \operatorname{Hom}_K(V, K)$ and dual basis $\{v_1^*, \dots, v_n^*\}$ satisfying $v_i^*(v_j) = \delta_{ij}$.
    - (Page 54) Define the determinant $\det(A) = \sum_{\sigma \in S_n} \operatorname{sgn}(\sigma) \prod_{i=1}^n A_{i, \sigma(i)}$ as the unique alternating multilinear $n$-form on $K^n$ normalized by $\det(I_n) = 1$.
    - (Page 62) State Cramer's rule and the adjugate formula $A \operatorname{adj}(A) = \det(A) I_n$.
    - (Page 65) Define the characteristic polynomial $P_A(\lambda) = \det(\lambda I_n - A)$ and minimal polynomial $\mu_A(\lambda)$.
    - (Page 81) State and prove the Cayley-Hamilton Theorem: every square matrix satisfies its own characteristic polynomial, $P_A(A) = 0$.
    - (Page 86) State the Jordan-Chevalley decomposition: every endomorphism $T$ on a vector space over an algebraically closed field decomposes uniquely as $T = T_s + T_n$ where $T_s$ is diagonalizable (semisimple), $T_n$ is nilpotent, and $T_s T_n = T_n T_s$.
    - (Page 87) State the Jordan Normal Form theorem for matrices over an algebraically closed field.
    - (Page 94) Define non-degenerate symmetric bilinear forms and the orthogonal complement $W^\perp = \{v \in V \mid f(v, w) = 0 \text{ for all } w \in W\}$, showing $\dim W + \dim W^\perp = \dim V$.
    - (Page 114) State the Spectral Theorem: every Hermitian matrix $A \in M_n(\mathbb{C})$ is unitarily diagonalizable with real eigenvalues ($U^* A U = D$).

https://www.math.ens.psl.eu/~benoist/refs/Eisenbud.pdf, Eisenbud's Commutative Algebra
