https://www.its.caltech.edu/~matilde/ICMtalkMarcolliSlides.pdf, noncommutative geometry in arithmetic
    - (Slide 3) Describe complex multiplication (CM) for elliptic curves $E_\tau = \mathbb{C}/(\mathbb{Z} + \tau \mathbb{Z})$ where $\operatorname{End}(E_\tau) \otimes \mathbb{Q} \cong K = \mathbb{Q}(\tau)$ is an imaginary quadratic field.
    - (Slide 4) State the main theorem of complex multiplication: the $j$-invariant $j(\tau)$ generates the Hilbert class field $H = K(j(\tau))$ of $K$, and abelian extensions are generated by $j(\tau)$ and torsion coordinates.
    - (Slide 6) Define the noncommutative torus $A_\theta$ as the universal $C^*$-algebra generated by unitaries $U, V$ satisfying $V U = e^{2\pi i \theta} U V$.

https://www.math.fsu.edu/~marcolli/NCGNTslides.pdf, noncommutative geometry in arithmetic focusing on Shimura varieties

https://www.andrej.com/assets/slides/laughable-theorems.pdf, Lawvere's fixed point theorem
    - (Slide 9) State and prove Lawvere's Fixed Point Theorem: if there exists a point-surjective morphism $e: A \to Y^A$ in a Cartesian closed category, then every endomorphism $f: Y \to Y$ has a fixed point $y \in Y$ such that $f(y) = y$. Deduce Cantor's theorem, Russell's paradox, and Gödel's incompleteness theorem as corollaries.

